    {
      "id": "primitives-landing",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Twilite Primitive Library",
      "position": {
        "x": -1000,
        "y": -900
      },
      "width": 800,
      "height": 540,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "2432f22c-5e03-4b80-a757-7233ff346477"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Twilite Language Library\n\nOne canonical graph per language contract. Meaning and representation are separate: an Edge is a relationship instance, Surface is a named chain, and neither requires a new node type. Edge Class is a definition graph; Event Handler, Method and membrane controls have node encodings.\n\nRead Specification Form first. Every spec separates agreed semantics, implementation evidence, known gaps and unresolved decisions. Structural validation is not proof of browser or runtime completeness. Advanced behavior (Bridge, Event Handler, Method, Script) is documented without making it mandatory starter structure.\n\nNode Class, Template and Collection are composed graphs; Property is class-backed; Workspace is a host composition and Minimap is a presentation. They can receive pattern specifications without being inferred as new primitives from registry membership.\n\nCanonical authority is github://mikemartinez1974/public/primitives/root.node. Other collections should point here through Portals, not create independently editable mirrors.\n\n## Compatibility and utilities\n\nThe registry is not the primitive list. markdown and svg remain readable legacy material nodes; new authoring uses Content. manifest is a compatibility declaration renderer. subscription is a legacy event-model node; new subscriptions belong to import Bridges and Event Handlers. dictionary and legend are class/discovery or inspection infrastructure, not new material primitives. div, embed, canvas and target are host/rendering or navigation facilities. timer, toggle, counter, gate, delay and api are utility nodes; availability is not a reason to classify them as fundamental language concepts.\n\nSource evidence: components/GraphEditor/nodeTypeRegistry.js and utils/nodeVisibilityRoles.js. Classification does not remove any registered type or claim that every utility is obsolete."
        }
      }
    },
